//
// The relabel — plan 09 phase C2.
//
// One hex, two names.  Every coordinate dryopea has ever written to
// disk is an **axial flat-top** label (`world.loft`); every
// coordinate it will write from now on is a **pointy-top odd-r
// offset** label (`lattice.loft`).  This file is the bijection
// between them, and C5 is what runs it over the real maps, markers
// and `.keys` scripts.
//
// ── Why it is the STANDARD conversion and not something bespoke ──
//
// Flat-top versus pointy-top is a RENDERING difference, not a graph
// one: axial coordinates have the same six neighbour offsets either
// way, and only `hex_to_*` differs.  So dryopea's old axial labels
// and the new lattice's axial view are the same graph, and the
// relabel is just axial → odd-r offset — `lat_from_axial`, which
// `hex_grid` already uses internally.
//
// ⚠ That is a CLAIM, not an axiom.  `tests/09_c2_relabel.loft`
// proves it, and proves it in the strong form: the relabel preserves
// DISTANCE, not merely adjacency.  Distance is what makes "a wall
// ring stays a wall ring" true, and it is what
// [`CLAUDE.md`](../CLAUDE.md) § the neighbour relation is promising
// when it says converting the table moves no distance in plan 11's
// flow field.
//
// ── The direction permutation is DERIVED, not chosen ────────────
//
// dryopea's six directions are axial deltas (`0` = `(0, +1)`, south
// on the old screen); `hex_grid`'s are compass points on a
// parity-dependent table.  Feed one through the relabel and ask
// `lat_direction` what came out, and the answer is the same at every
// cell and on both row parities:
//
//     old 0 1 2 3 4 5
//     new 5 0 1 2 3 4      i.e. new = (old + 5) % 6
//
// Measured before it was written down, at 25 cells × 6 directions,
// and the test re-derives it over the whole ±16 window rather than
// trusting this comment.
//
// ⚠ **The picture changes by more than a mirror.**  § Open questions
// 2 accepted "let the maps flip", and the flip is real — but
// flat-top → pointy-top is ITSELF a re-orientation of the hexagon,
// so the composite is a mirror AND one 60° hex rotation.  Old
// direction 0 pointed SOUTH on the old screen; its relabel is new
// direction 5, which renders NORTH-EAST.  Nothing is wrong; it is
// simply more than "upside down", and a reviewer comparing a
// converted map against an old screenshot needs to know that before
// they go looking for a bug.
//
// ── Lifetime ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Separate from `lattice.loft` on purpose.  The lattice is
// permanent; this is migration, and how long it lives is C5's open
// decision — either the loader migrates old saves on read (and this
// stays forever) or the files are converted once and old ones
// refused (and this can go).  Keeping it in its own file means that
// decision is a file-level one.

use lattice;

// An old axial flat-top label → the new odd-r offset label for the
// same hex.
pub fn relabel_hex(old: const Hex) -> Hex {
    lat_from_axial(old.q, old.r)
}

// …and back, for reading a converted file with old-lattice code
// (and for the round-trip the gate rests on).
pub fn relabel_hex_back(cur: const Hex) -> Hex {
    rhb_a = lat_to_axial(cur);
    Hex { q: rhb_a.0, r: rhb_a.1 }
}

// An old direction index (0..5) → the new one naming the same step.
//
// Derived, not chosen — see the header.  Out-of-range input is
// folded rather than rejected, because the one caller that matters
// is C5 reading a `direction: u8` off disk, where a corrupt value
// should land on a real direction instead of failing a whole map.
pub fn relabel_direction(d: integer) -> integer {
    rd_n = d % 6;
    if rd_n < 0 { rd_n += 6; }
    (rd_n + 5) % 6
}

pub fn relabel_direction_back(d: integer) -> integer {
    rdb_n = d % 6;
    if rdb_n < 0 { rdb_n += 6; }
    (rdb_n + 1) % 6
}
